1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,100
Before David Hufford became a folklorist,

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,660
he woke to the sound of his bedroom door
opening.

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,660
Footsteps crossed the room.

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:08,540
His body would not move.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:10,040
The mattress sank.

6
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:12,180
Weight pressed into his chest.

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,980
Years later, strangers in Newfoundland
described the same sequence and

8
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,560
gave the visitor a name Hufford said he
had never heard:

9
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:21,000
the Old Hag.

10
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:24,840
Researchers can make the paralysis more
likely and see its

11
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:26,760
traces on a monitor.

12
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,720
But why does a body caught between
dreaming and waking

13
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:32,480
so often feel as though someone else has
entered the room?

14
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:35,340
Hufford later described more than fear.

15
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,990
He felt intention: the certainty that
whatever was above him

16
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,500
was hostile.

17
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:41,740
Then the pressure vanished.

18
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:43,050
Movement returned.

19
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,140
The room was ordinary again.

20
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:48,280
But the experience was too structured to
feel like a

21
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:49,900
random fragment of a dream:

22
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:52,800
the opening door, the approaching steps,

23
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:54,560
the body that would not answer,

24
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,200
the weight on his chest.

25
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:58,940
At the time, he had no name for it.

26
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:01,880
He did not know that this sequence was
already living

27
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:03,880
inside another community's stories.

28
00:01:04,519 --> 00:01:08,040
And that turns one frightening night into
a much stranger question.

29
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:11,540
Can an experience reach us before the
story that is

30
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:12,700
supposed to explain it?

31
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:17,580
Years later, Hufford worked with local
accounts and archives in Newfoundland.

32
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,830
On Fogo Island, he encountered an
expression that sounded almost

33
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:24,500
casual: to have the Old Hag.

34
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:27,850
A person wakes in a familiar room. They
cannot move.

35
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:29,370
Something approaches.

36
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,420
Breathing becomes difficult.

37
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:33,600
A weight settles onto the chest.

38
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:37,080
And through it all comes the absolute
conviction that this

39
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:38,740
is happening while they are awake.

40
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,400
For Hufford, the resemblance was
uncomfortably precise.

41
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:46,000
He later collected accounts from other
people who,

42
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:48,780
he reported, had not known the tradition
beforehand.

43
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:53,580
In 1982, that work became The Terror That
Comes in the Night.

44
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:56,800
Perhaps culture gives the experience a
face.

45
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:00,300
But what if the experience sometimes comes
first?

46
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:02,700
Every night during REM sleep,

47
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:06,160
the brain restrains much of the body's
voluntary movement.

48
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:08,729
It is a protective silence built into
dreaming:

49
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:11,940
the dream can run while the body remains
still.

50
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:15,340
Usually, awareness and movement return
together.

51
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,120
In sleep paralysis, the handover slips out
of sequence.

52
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:23,260
Your mind reaches the room before movement
reaches your muscles.

53
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:24,950
You know where you are.

54
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:26,290
You can see the door.

55
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:27,970
You can hear the room around you.

56
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:30,109
The command to move is real,

57
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:31,900
but the arm does not respond.

58
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:36,549
Breathing continues, yet the chest can
feel unfamiliar or restricted,

59
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:40,930
especially when fear turns every sensation
into urgent information.

60
00:02:41,610 --> 00:02:43,200
This explains the paralysis.

61
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:45,549
It does not yet explain the footsteps.

62
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:47,590
When psychologist J.

63
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:51,109
Allan Cheyne and his colleagues studied
these experiences,

64
00:02:51,730 --> 00:02:54,100
the reports did not scatter randomly.

65
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:56,530
They repeatedly formed three broad
families.

66
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:59,320
First comes the Intruder: a sound,

67
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,900
a shadow, footsteps, the sense that
someone is present.

68
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,140
Then the Incubus: pressure, choking,

69
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:08,880
weight, something holding the body down.

70
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,780
And in the third family, the body itself
loses its

71
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:13,769
usual boundaries.

72
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:17,000
People feel themselves floating, falling,

73
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:19,720
turning, or looking back at the place
where their body

74
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:20,920
should be.

75
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:22,470
A visitor. An attack.

76
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:24,109
An escape from the body.

77
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:28,470
Why does one unstable border between sleep
and waking keep

78
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:30,350
opening into these particular rooms?

79
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:37,120
In 1992, Japanese sleep researchers worked
with sixteen healthy participants.

80
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:39,260
Their question was restrained but
important:

81
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:42,940
could sleep paralysis become more likely
if sleep and REM

82
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:44,320
were deliberately disrupted?

83
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:46,680
Participants were allowed to sleep,

84
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:50,080
awakened at selected times, kept awake for
an hour,

85
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:53,360
and then sent back to bed while
researchers monitored brain

86
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,600
activity and muscle tone.

87
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:57,060
It did not happen on command.

88
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:58,860
It did not happen to everyone.

89
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:01,750
But under those interrupted-sleep
conditions,

90
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:05,140
six episodes of isolated sleep paralysis
occurred.

91
00:04:05,790 --> 00:04:07,620
The people knew they were in a laboratory.

92
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:10,790
They were conscious enough to recognize
their surroundings,

93
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:13,240
yet their bodies remained immobilized.

94
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:18,940
Most of those episodes also included
disturbing visual or auditory experiences.

95
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:22,890
Their reports and polysomnograms captured
the contradiction:

96
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:26,340
waking awareness while muscular atonia
persisted.

97
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,540
The doorway into the state was measurable.

98
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,760
What stepped through it felt deeply
personal.

99
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:34,830
Before we consider why a presence appears,

100
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:38,300
put yourself inside the moment. You wake
tonight.

101
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:40,380
Your bedroom door is closed.

102
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,300
Your arm will not move.

103
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:45,000
Then you hear two steps beside the bed.

104
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:48,600
The air feels occupied before you can see
any shape.

105
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:52,280
Which detail would convince you first that
someone was there:

106
00:04:52,870 --> 00:04:55,700
the pressure, the sound, or the presence
itself?

107
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:58,480
Leave one word below: PRESSURE,

108
00:04:58,800 --> 00:04:59,900
SOUND, or PRESENCE.

109
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:03,260
And if this has happened to you, tell us
which came first.

110
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:06,940
Because for some people, the presence
arrives before the image.

111
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:09,620
That may be the strangest part of all.

112
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:12,680
One plausible explanation begins with
alarm.

113
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:15,700
You are conscious enough to know that
something is wrong.

114
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:17,250
You cannot move.

115
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:19,650
Your breathing feels unfamiliar.

116
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,540
Yet there is no visible cause.

117
00:05:22,251 --> 00:05:23,700
The mind is left with danger,

118
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:25,020
but no source for it.

119
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:29,540
In that condition, a perceptual system may
begin searching for an agent.

120
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:32,580
A small sound gains the rhythm of a
footstep.

121
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:35,440
A coat and a doorway align into a
shoulder.

122
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:39,580
Pressure gains direction, then weight,
then intention.

123
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:42,660
The brain does not merely record a room.

124
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,099
It continuously predicts what the room
contains.

125
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:48,230
Under fear and incomplete information,

126
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:52,120
those predictions can become intensely
specific.

127
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:55,400
This offers a route by which ambiguity may
acquire a body.

128
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,380
But it leaves a more intimate question
untouched.

129
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:01,960
Why do some people feel watched before
they see anything at all?

130
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:04,440
In some accounts, there is no face.

131
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:07,950
No outline. Sometimes there is not even a
sound.

132
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:11,860
There is only the certainty that another
awareness is in the room.

133
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:15,090
It is waiting, observing, or moving
closer.

134
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:17,300
The figure may appear later,

135
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:20,100
almost as if the mind is giving shape to
something

136
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:21,700
it already believes is there.

137
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:25,810
Research can classify the pattern and test
mechanisms behind it.

138
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:29,730
But it cannot inhabit every subjective
second of the experience.

139
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,550
That may be why sleep paralysis never
remained only a

140
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:35,240
sleep disorder.

141
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:37,410
An invisible cause receives a name.

142
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:39,190
The name becomes a story.

143
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:42,720
The story changes what the next person
expects to meet.

144
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:45,580
And then the encounter feeds the story
again.

145
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:48,160
So return to Hufford's original problem.

146
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:51,960
His body may have been caught between REM
sleep and waking.

147
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:54,580
That explains why he could not move.

148
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:58,460
The Old Hag tradition may have supplied a
cultural form

149
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:00,640
for similar experiences in Newfoundland.

150
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:04,750
But Hufford's question survives because he
said he encountered the

151
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:06,860
pattern before he encountered its name.

152
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:10,160
What if folklore does not merely invent
beings?

153
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:12,320
What if, at least sometimes,

154
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,120
it preserves the recurring shape of an
experience:

155
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:17,500
an experience produced where body,

156
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:19,700
perception, and expectation meet?

157
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:22,060
The physiology opens the door.

158
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:24,400
Culture gives the darkness a face.

159
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:26,350
And once that face exists,

160
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:28,400
it may enter the next person's night.

161
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:32,880
In 1692, a private night entered the
public record.

162
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:36,460
A witness in Salem later described a woman
entering a

163
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:38,780
locked room, pressing onto his chest,

164
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,200
and leaving him unable to speak or move.

165
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:43,300
Her name was Bridget Bishop.

166
00:07:43,951 --> 00:07:46,480
The experience became part of the case
against her.

167
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:49,010
In June, she was tried, convicted,

168
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:50,420
and hanged for witchcraft.

169
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:53,800
In Part Two, we follow the visitor from
the bedroom

170
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:57,220
into the courtroom and ask what happens
when an experience

171
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:00,900
no one else can see becomes evidence
against another human being.

172
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:03,820
Subscribe if you want to continue that
trail.

173
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:06,000
And before you leave: PRESSURE,

174
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:07,440
SOUND, or PRESENCE.

175
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:09,520
Which would convince you first?

176
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:10,960
The body wakes.

177
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:12,920
The story keeps moving.
